Title: Deliver curated embedder registry invariant first
Created: 2026-08-30
Author: Minh Ha-Duong

--- log ---
2026-08-30T14:50Z Minh Ha-Duong created

2026-08-31T06:00Z claude note the 2026-08-31 ruling on the swap's motivation (spec/DECISIONS.md) lands on child 0495 only: R7 conformance is the first filter in the ship gate, not one score among several. Stages 0489-0494 are unaffected -- the invariant-first order and the parity proofs do not depend on why the swap happens
2026-09-02T11:07Z claude note first child 0489 done on fork branch t0489-minilm-singleton-entry (8d2893c, 4b2e32e): the incumbent chain is one registry entry, vectors and vector key byte-identical; three fields authoritative, seven declared for 0490
2026-09-03T08:01Z R31 is now asserted: R31-configure-proves-or-fails-loudly in bench/acceptance/assertions.py. It grades the order rather than an exception — a configure that returns without complaint followed by a query the configured path cannot answer is the red; a configure that fails loudly is the clause's other branch and is green. Fail-control is stub-configures-blind, whose query raises only once configure has been called, so the red names one cause instead of also failing R13's both-answer clause. Stated limit, carried in the docstring: a target that validates nothing and happens to work here is green, because offering a configuration known to be unusable means naming a target's own configuration surface, which the contract puts in the adapter. The invariant-first implementation is still this ticket's.
2026-09-03T08:29Z Review round on PR #255 reshaped R31: the red is now read from the target's own status (embedder not active after configure returned), not from an exception. The first version graded a raising configure green and a raising query red — the layer cannot tell a refusal from a dead transport, so one reading manufactured a green out of a broken instrument and the other a red out of the same event one verb later. Both are now not-run. install runs first where offered. stub-configures-blind expresses the defect by report rather than by raising; it also reddens R10-local-by-default, kept because R10's own falsifier names a local embedder configured but not running.
2026-09-03T08:43Z R31's assertion is WITHDRAWN, and the reason is a finding rather than a defect to patch. Reviewed twice: the red condition it could observe (embedder not active after configure returned) is a strict subset of R10-local-by-default's, so it reddened exactly where R10 already reddened and nowhere else — no discriminating power, a green about nobody wearing R31's number. The clause is about WHEN validation happens; everything the seven verbs expose after configure returns is a fact about the target's current state. Deciding it needs a way to offer a configuration known to be unusable here and watch when the target notices, and configure() takes no argument (SPEC.md 5.2.8, ratified). That is a contract question for the author, and this ticket carries it. bench/acceptance/assertions.py records the reasoning where the assertion would have gone; README's ladder row now says undecidable rather than asserted.
